;; ===========================================================================
;; MUST-FAIL FIXTURE — a later change / the operator runbook an internal review a finding.
;;
;; `lock-tranche` was a public defun gated only by `(require-capability (ADMIN))`.
;; Its docstring said "PRIVATE (init-supply only)", which is a comment, not a
;; property — Pact has no private functions. So whenever an admin signature was
;; present in a transaction, a foreign module composed ADMIN and called it.
;;
;; MEASURED BEFORE THE FIX, and this fixture is the NAME half of the proof: with
;; `env-keys ["admin-key"]` present, the plant returned "tranche locked", a row
;; `"evil"` with a 999,999.0 total was readable, and a forged TRANCHE-LOCKED was
;; emitted — on the event the module itself designates "the public disclosure
;; anchor". This file loads successfully against that module.
;;
;; 🔴 A NAME-PIN IS NOT THE WHOLE PROOF, AND THAT IS WHY THERE IS A SECOND HALF.
;; Audit #6's lead finding was a deleted name pinned exactly like this while a
;; foreign module composed the ENCLOSING CAPABILITY and reached the same writes.
;; The ROUTE half lives in `a probe kept out of this repo`, which composes ADMIN —
;; the destination's own capability — and measures what it can still reach.
;; Read them as one proof; neither is sufficient alone.
;;
;; If this file ever starts SUCCEEDING again, a named tranche writer has returned.
;; ===========================================================================
(load "setup.repl")

(begin-tx "the attacker: compose ADMIN and reach for the named writer")
(env-data { "ak": { "keys": ["ltattacker"], "pred": "keys-all" } })
(env-keys ["ltattacker"])
(define-namespace "ltns" (read-keyset 'ak) (read-keyset 'ak))
(namespace "ltns")
(module ltrob G
 ;; Real governance: the attack composes SPT's ADMIN-GOV, not this module's own gate.
 ;; 🔴 the design record (a later change): this MUST name a cap that EXISTS. Left as the retired `ADMIN`, the
 ;; module fails to resolve the cap and the fixture aborts on the CAP name instead of on
 ;; `lock-tranche` — passing run-tests.sh's must-fail check while asserting nothing about the
 ;; deleted function. Measured: that is exactly what happened before this line was updated.
  (defcap G () (enforce-guard (read-keyset 'ak)))
  (defcap A () (compose-capability (n_48867b242317a0216a67f8c7ca26696b5878e0e3.SPT.ADMIN-GOV)))
  (defun plant:string (t:string b:string g:guard tot:decimal)
    (with-capability (A)
 ;; <<< THE NAME THE ATTACKER REACHES FOR — must not exist >>>
      (n_48867b242317a0216a67f8c7ca26696b5878e0e3.SPT.lock-tranche
        t b g tot (n_48867b242317a0216a67f8c7ca26696b5878e0e3.SPT.curr-time) 1 2))))
(commit-tx)

(begin-tx "the plant, riding a transaction that already carries an admin signature")
(env-data { "eg": { "keys": ["evilkey"], "pred": "keys-all" } })
(env-keys ["admin-key"])
;; NO `try` HERE, DELIBERATELY. `try` forces a read-only context, so wrapping a
;; write-path attack in it makes the insert fail with "Operation disallowed in
;; read-only or sys-only mode" — which reads exactly like a block and is an
;; artifact. That false negative appeared in an internal review's own first probe and again
;; in mine while writing this fixture. Never wrap a write attack in `try`.
(ltns.ltrob.plant "evil" (create-principal (read-keyset 'eg)) (read-keyset 'eg) 999999.0)
;; Non-vacuity: against the UNFIXED module the plant succeeds and this read answers
;; from a row nobody authorized, so the file exits 0 and run-tests.sh reports the
;; attack live again.
(let ((r (n_48867b242317a0216a67f8c7ca26696b5878e0e3.SPT.tranche-releasable "evil")))
  (print (format "PLANTED ROW READS BACK: tranche-releasable evil = {}" [r])))
(commit-tx)
